  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and seasonable weather continues through the\nweek. The first day of November is the next opportunity for\nrain.\n\nHigh pressure remains over the Mid-Atlantic and Southeast \nstates through the end of the month. This high amplitude \nanticyclone will flatten in response to short wave energy \nmoving through the faster flow to our north. Mid and upper level\nmoisture will increase Wednesday, remnants of the storm system \nwhich plagued the Southwest, but is weakening as it moves \nnortheast. Its passage will bring a veil of cirrus but nothing \nmore. We may actually end of with some marine strato-cu as the \nsurface winds become easterly late tonight into Wednesday...the\nHREF the most bullish with respect to this cloud layer. Depending\non cloud thickness, this may impact temperatures with lower \nhigh temps Wednesday and higher low temps Wednesday night.\n\nAside from the passage of the shortwave trough Wednesday the\noverall ridge of high pressure will remain in place for the\nremainder of the week and into the weekend. Temperatures should\nremain seasonably mild through the end of the month as a result.\nFriday looks to be the warmest day with high temperatures 10\ndegrees above normal.\n\nAs for the next chance of rain. Models have been consistent with\nadvertisement of a southern stream short wave to cross the\ncountry over the weekend, bringing moisture into the area\nbeginning Sunday November 1st. Some rain may sneak in here late\nHalloween night, but all models still insist on dry conditions\nfor the Trick-or-Treat crowd Saturday evening.\n\nStrength of the initial wave Sunday appears weak, but models do\nindicate southeast upslope flow which should enhance the rain \nchance along the Blue Ridge. A series of disturbances are \nforecast to follow during the week, so it looks promising for \nadditional rainfall, and this should help snuff the late \nOctober dry spell.",
